1
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:09,316
The MoU trap. Episode 2. What an MoU really binds

2
00:00:12,066 --> 00:00:15,671
Part two. What an MoU binds, and what it does not.

3
00:00:18,521 --> 00:00:30,638
A memorandum of understanding records an intention to cooperate. On its central commitment, most MoUs bind no one. Neither side is obliged to build, to buy, or to pay.

4
00:00:33,388 --> 00:00:37,804
But the legal reading understates what an MoU does in practice.

5
00:00:38,304 --> 00:00:43,083
Exclusivity over a site or a resource, which keeps competitors out.

6
00:00:43,633 --> 00:00:49,051
An indicative tariff, which becomes the reference point for every later negotiation.

7
00:00:49,601 --> 00:00:53,847
Promises of state help, expected before anyone has priced them.

8
00:00:54,397 --> 00:01:00,925
And a public signing: a political commitment that is harder to withdraw than any clause in the document.

9
00:01:02,875 --> 00:01:05,499
Belpau: The MoU says the tariff is only indicative.

10
00:01:05,799 --> 00:01:08,935
Enilec Mok: Every negotiation after today will start from that number.

11
00:01:10,685 --> 00:01:13,458
Three stages. Three kinds of commitment.

12
00:01:14,258 --> 00:01:19,677
At the MoU stage, little binds in law. The state gives a political signal.

13
00:01:20,277 --> 00:01:27,040
At the contract stage: tariff, term and obligations, with future payments conditional on operation.

14
00:01:27,640 --> 00:01:34,296
At financial close, it is all funded, and the state's obligations are fixed for the life of the contract.

15
00:01:37,346 --> 00:01:40,738
The trap lies between the first stage and the third.

16
00:01:41,838 --> 00:01:50,670
Some commitments never close. Others do close, on terms first sketched when the project was least tested, and the government least prepared.

17
00:01:53,220 --> 00:02:00,516
An MoU, and often the first contract after it, is signed before anyone has applied either of two tests.

18
00:02:01,016 --> 00:02:04,237
The lender's test: can the project repay its debt?

19
00:02:04,787 --> 00:02:12,617
The state's test: can the power system and the public finances carry the project's obligations for twenty years or more?

20
00:02:14,817 --> 00:02:17,462
Next episode: Why projects stall.
